<<~ ahu slot=`#/b3-capstack-stages-collide-2026-10-31-narrow-bri`>>

! b3-capstack-stages collide (Anvil-Three, Sonnet), 2026-10-31. Narrow brief: (a) compose order, (b) floor vs systemCanon, F5 seats. No internet research run (not asked in the narrow brief); no outside sources cited.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/new-findings`>>

!! New findings
None live. One order-pin gap (below, N1).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/composecorevessel-call-order-confirmed-safe-by-a`>>

!! (a) composeCoreVessel call order: CONFIRMED safe, by array position AND dependency
* core-caps.ts:241-267: stack = [substrate, wikislot, daemon, wiki, pool, mount, ...extra]. cap-compose.ts:61-120 visits in stack order (DFS, deps first), builds sequentially with await. Order: substrate, wikislot, daemon, wiki, pool, mount, extras. composeHerm (node-caps.ts:191) also lists substrate first, daemon second.
* substrateCap.build = assembleVessel(keel) (core-caps.ts:100); assembleVessel awaits keel.systemCanon at open-vessel-core.ts:191, before any other cap builds.
* The line order in open-node-vessel.ts differs and does not matter: `standSyncGates` is DEFINED at 1514 (before systemCanon at 1973) but INVOKED at 3272-3273 inside openDaemon (2034), which only daemonCap.build calls (core-caps.ts:121), after substrate. The dial (2339) and sibling channel (2669) also sit inside openDaemon.
* N1 (pin gap): nothing I found asserts this order. A future extraCap with no `requires` still builds after substrate only because it is listed after it; reordering the array would not fail. Red-first: a composeCoreVessel/composeHerm test recording build order, asserting systemCanon fired before openDaemon's first call (control: a stack with daemon listed first still builds substrate first via `requires`; the pin guards the Herm/extras). Cheap, CUT-READY ahead of T11.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/floor-not-fail-open-finding-f1-stays-latent-not`>>

!! (b) Floor (sovereign=null): NOT fail-open. Finding F1 stays LATENT, not security-live
* keel.systemCanon is defined unconditionally (open-node-vessel.ts:1973), so a floor runs it. With custodyGroup null (custodyGroup derives from `sovereign ?` at prepareNodeBootOn), standKahuCanon returns {} at once (kahu-realm.ts:269-271); the holder still stands (1986-1989) and recounts, so kahuProtected is non-null (empty ids) before openDaemon.
* No peer can arrive at the floor before then, and few after: standSyncGates returns early when `!sovereign` (1514-1521): no gate, no registerUpgrade, so no inbound wire peer at all. The sibling channel needs personaGroupId (2655), absent at floor. The only floor ingress is the outbound dial (2339), in openDaemon after substrate; a dialed hearth reads weakest (dialRelation, shelf lane). I took the shelf lane's closure from the wave-1 walk, not re-read.
* Narrowing worth a note, not a fault: protection follows the canon a vessel NAMES. A vessel that names none (floor, or sovereign but faceless/outside the cabal) protects none, by design (kahu-realm.ts:262-264). A floor booted on a store holding Kahu docs from an earlier sovereign run reads them unclassed; it is reachable only by a dialed peer on the shelf lane. Ask the controller if that wants a ruling; I did not prove a leak.
* Ruling: no security-class finding. T11's cell-tightening lean stands as defense in depth (null cell = withhold), red-first.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/f5-settle-the-conflict`>>

!! F5: settle the conflict
* peerContractNymMap: REFUTED as a burn candidate. Nym-map scout stands. Declared 876, readers live (1366, 2226, nexus-carriage.ts:567/584), fails closed (empty map -> null nym -> stranger). Held by membership-doctrine.mem #/two-maps clause 3 (operator-ruled: realm consult reads a separate ROOT map) and road :251 (house ruling 2026-10-16, flips only if a root edge proven at the reading seat fills a realm map). Wave 1 conflated "no writer today" with "dead". Old-shapes-burn does not reach an unborn seat the operator ruled.
* walkerBoard: NARROWED. Production never passes it (only self-slot-share.test.ts:214) and walker arms ship shut (open-node-vessel.ts:3267-3270), but the lane is the reader half of the owed E walker arm: road :492 (H8, grant public-half verifier specified in walker-grant.ts), :751 and :774 ("before E's walker arm"). A "walker" class sorter exists (face-gate.ts:463, socket-sorter.ts:97, hosting-mint/carry). Not old shape; unborn. Do NOT burn with T11. The branch fails closed (carriage-board walker read returns false, 122).
* Rule: separate DEAD (old shape, no seat) from UNBORN (seat held by canon/road). Both here are unborn. Cure is wording: restate 873-875 and 3267-3270 as law with the owed row named ("a root edge proven at the reading seat fills this"; "E's walker arm wires walkerBoard"), per nym scout.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/verdicts`>>

!! Verdicts
* T11 stays NEEDS-RULING; F5 burn lean DROPPED for both. Tighten null cells red-first stays the lean. Add N1 pin as its first red.

---

<<~/ahu>>

